<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Lament · c. 1900 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Dle Yaman</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hy">Դլե Յաման</bdi> / <bdi lang="hy-Latn">Dle Yaman</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old Armenian folk song about a sad love story, written down by the priest and musician Komitas on his travels.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional (re-arranged by Komitas)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (arranged for piano by Komitas)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Armenians</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Armenia, in the countryside, where Komitas collected it</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional; collected by Komitas</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional words, arranged by Komitas (died 1935))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Dle Yaman&quot; is a traditional Armenian tune that tells the story of a tragic love affair between two people. It was discovered by Father Komitas during his travels in the Armenian countryside. Komitas re-arranged both its words and its music for the piano. In Western Armenian spelling the title is written Տըլէ Եաման, and in French it is often given as &quot;Délé Yaman&quot;.</p><p>The song became one of the best-known Armenian songs, with hundreds of interpretations and covers. It is very often performed with solo passages on the duduk. Among the best-known versions are those of the soprano Lusine Zakaryan, of Levon Minassian, of Djivan Gasparyan on the duduk, of Flora Martirosian, and of Nune Yesayan with Jivan Gasparyan. Singers outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> have taken it up as well, among them the Canadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Isabel</a> Bayrakdarian, the French singers Hélène Segara and Patrick Fiori, and the Russian singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a>.</p><p>Over time the song came to be sung as one of the hymns of the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, which is commemorated every year on 24 April. The French-Armenian director Henri Verneuil made it the main music of his semi-autobiographical film Mayrig (1991).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A folk song of love that ends in sorrow, told as the story of two people whose love goes wrong. Its slow, grieving mood is why it later came to be sung in remembrance of the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
</div>
